WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.740
YURI GRINSHTEYN: We've covered
using Cloud Trace to measure

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.870
latency on our
services and OpenCensus

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.110
to collect custom metro
telemetry already.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:07.860
But what if you could
use a single library

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.330
to accomplish both?

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.760
Today, I'll introduce
you to OpenTelemetry,

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.740
the next major
release of OpenCensus,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.990
and show you how to use it
for distributed tracing.

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:17.420
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.620
As our applications become more
complex and more services are

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.672
involved in serving user traffic
and completing transactions,

00:00:30.672 --> 00:00:32.630
it becomes more and more
critical to understand

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.700
how requests
traverse our services

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.530
and how each service
contributes to overall latency.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.650
This is what distributed
tracing does.

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.780
It captures the latency
of user requests

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:44.060
and how long it takes each
microservice in the path

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.750
to return a response.

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:48.530
The next release of OpenCensus
is merging the OpenTracing

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.890
project in a new open source
project called OpenTelemetry,

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:54.920
which provides a single set
of APIs, libraries, agents,

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.650
and collector services to
capture distributed traces

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:59.630
and metrics from
your application.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:02.300
OpenTelemetry is a Cloud Native
Computing Foundation sandbox

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:03.070
project.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.600
And I'm here today to show
you how to use it for tracing.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.293
Let's start by
reviewing the basics.

00:01:07.293 --> 00:01:09.710
These were originally described
in Google's research paper

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:11.730
on the Dapper Distributed
Tracing Framework.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:13.730
We'll link to it below
if you'd like to read it.

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.370
When a user request comes in,
we want to create a trace--

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:18.620
the total of information
that describes how our system

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.540
responds to a user request.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.720
Traces are composed of spans,
where each span represents

00:01:23.720 --> 00:01:26.090
a specific request and
response pair involved

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:27.620
in serving the user request.

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.320
The parent span describes the
latency as observed by the end

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.160
user.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:32.587
Each of the child
spans describes

00:01:32.587 --> 00:01:34.670
how a particular service
in the distributed system

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.580
was called and responded with
latency information captured

00:01:37.580 --> 00:01:38.730
for each.

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:40.312
Let's take a look
at a basic example.

00:01:40.312 --> 00:01:42.770
We have a front-end service
that responds the user requests

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.662
and makes calls to
a backend service.

00:01:44.662 --> 00:01:46.120
It has to wait on
that backend call

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.045
to complete to return the
response back to the user.

00:01:49.045 --> 00:01:50.420
We want to trace
this interaction

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.500
to understand both total latency
and latency for each service.

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.390
In the frontend code,
we start tracing

00:01:56.390 --> 00:01:57.927
and create the root span.

00:01:57.927 --> 00:01:59.510
We then make a request
to the back end

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.280
with the appropriate context.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.410
This is data embedded
in the HTTP headers

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:04.910
that our backend
can use to append

00:02:04.910 --> 00:02:07.010
subsequent spans to our trace.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.410
In the backend code,
we extract the context

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.740
on the incoming request and use
it to create additional spans.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:14.320
This allows us to
explicitly measure

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:16.310
the latency of our
backend service.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.470
Further, we can
create custom spans

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.290
if, for example, we want to time
how long specific backend tasks

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:22.550
might take.

00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.540
When we look at trace
in the cloud console,

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.832
we can see our trace captured
with detailed information

00:02:26.832 --> 00:02:29.480
about how long each step
in the interaction takes.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.387
OpenTelemetry takes
it a step further

00:02:31.387 --> 00:02:33.470
by exposing details about
the network interactions

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.770
between our services.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.610
We can even see how long it
took to do the DNS resolution,

00:02:37.610 --> 00:02:39.110
establish a connection.

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.540
I'm incredibly excited by the
promise of the OpenTelemetry

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:42.122
Project.

00:02:42.122 --> 00:02:43.580
Next time, we'll
take a look at how

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:45.920
to use OpenTelemetry to get
a custom metric telemetry

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.150
from our services.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.450
That's it for this episode.

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.640
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:50.660
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.010
